Peano's multigrid extension provides stand-alone multigrid solers. However, the
solver ingredients are designed in a way that they can be used by other codes, 
too.

# Configure PETSc

The first thing that the user needs to do is install PETSc somewhere on their system.
[The instructions on their website](https://petsc.org/release/install/download/), but 
we will summarise the steps here.

1. Clone their repo. It's best to do this somewhere away from the Peano directory.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
git clone -b release https://gitlab.com/petsc/petsc.git petsc
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

2. cd into this directory.

The next steps will depend on your setup, but we will give here some general tips.

3. Run the configure script inside the PETSc directory:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure --with-cc=(C-COMPILER) --with-cxx=(C++-COMPILER) --with-fc=0
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In this instance we used the intel C and CC compilers.

4. If this succeeds then you can make:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
make PETSC_DIR=/path/to/petsc PETSC_ARCH=system_architecture all
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

By default, system_architecture is arch-linux-c-debug, but this is something that can be
changed at the configure stage. 

It's worth adding PETSC_DIR and PETSC_ARCH to bashrc / bash_profile, as these will be used 
throughout.

## Reconfigure Peano

We will then need to reconfigure Peano. Change directories into the top level of Peano.

In addition to the --with-petsc and --enable-finiteelements flags,
we will need to make additions to CXXFLAGS and LDFLAGS to ensure that we can compile with PETSc.

In addition to whichever CXXFLAGS were present before, we should include:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
CXXFLAGS="(...) -I$PETSC_DIR/include -I$PETSC_DIR/$PETSC_ARCH/include"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

to ensure that the approprirate libraries are available to the compiler.

Moreover,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
LDFLAGS="(...) -lpetsc -Wl,-rpath,$PETSC_DIR/$PETSC_ARCH/lib -L$PETSC_DIR/$PETSC_ARCH/lib -L/path/to/gcc/libraries  "
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The first flag here comes from PETSc itself, and requires PETSC_DIR and PETSC_ARCH to be established already.
The last flag is the path to the gcc runtime libraries. As an example, on a local machine, this libary looks like:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-L/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

We can then put these together:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure ... --enable-finiteelements --with-petsc CXXFLAGS=$(CXXFLAGS) LDFLAGS=$(LDFLAGS)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


*/